  "text": "A recent study expands earlier work on how the electromagnetic field contributes to the inertia of a moving charged object. The author examines a perfectly conducting, flattened sphere whose long axis points along the direction of motion, and whose shape is characterized by a parameter equal to the ratio of its speed to the speed of light at the instant considered. The analysis yields new expressions for the effective longitudinal and transverse inertial coefficients, which differ from the classic formulas derived from the traditional mass‑energy approach. The technique avoids the need for the quasi‑steady‑state assumption normally required in energy‑based calculations, but it relies on specific initial hypotheses about the internal structure of the charge and the conditions at its surface. These built‑in assumptions restrict the general applicability of the model and invite criticism that it does not fully comply with the requirement that physical laws be invariant for all observers."
